1
00:00:08,434 --> 00:00:10,637
The moment and the atom are gone.

2
00:00:10,717 --> 00:00:12,408
Is space the last floor?

3
00:00:13,238 --> 00:00:16,025
I am John Tan, known as Thusness.

4
00:00:16,105 --> 00:00:20,296
These are my own notes, from my
own practice and contemplation.

5
00:00:21,101 --> 00:00:26,184
In Part 2, the habit retreated to the
smallest moment and the smallest piece of

6
00:00:26,264 --> 00:00:29,041
matter. And found no floor in either.

7
00:00:29,854 --> 00:00:32,655
The habit has lost the
moment and the atom.

8
00:00:32,735 --> 00:00:34,711
It has one more place to stand.

9
00:00:35,509 --> 00:00:38,703
Surely, it says, space at least is real.

10
00:00:38,783 --> 00:00:42,100
One still, empty room, in which the bell,

11
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:44,301
the flower and I all sit.

12
00:00:44,381 --> 00:00:46,733
It was there before
anything was put in it.

13
00:00:46,813 --> 00:00:49,808
And it would still be there
if everything were taken out.

14
00:00:50,609 --> 00:00:54,079
Or, and it is the biggest
or, look for that room.

15
00:00:54,898 --> 00:00:59,601
The eighth century Indian master
Śāntarakṣita built his whole Ornament of

16
00:00:59,681 --> 00:01:01,874
the Middle Way on one test.

17
00:01:02,716 --> 00:01:06,834
Here is the rule. Whatever is
really there, from its own side,

18
00:01:06,914 --> 00:01:10,127
must be either really one, or really many.

19
00:01:10,925 --> 00:01:14,309
Really one means a single
whole, with no parts.

20
00:01:14,389 --> 00:01:17,557
Really many means a collection
of such single wholes.

21
00:01:17,637 --> 00:01:19,364
There is no third choice.

22
00:01:19,444 --> 00:01:21,538
And many is not a separate escape.

23
00:01:21,618 --> 00:01:24,907
Many only means several
ones, counted together.

24
00:01:24,987 --> 00:01:29,395
So if no real one can be found,
no real many can be found either.

25
00:01:30,201 --> 00:01:32,580
The whole test comes down to one question.

26
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:34,419
Can a real one be found?

27
00:01:35,221 --> 00:01:38,646
Is space one? There is
space here, around the bell.

28
00:01:38,726 --> 00:01:41,065
And space over there, around the gate.

29
00:01:41,145 --> 00:01:45,784
Is the space around the bell the very
same space as the space around the gate?

30
00:01:46,601 --> 00:01:51,263
If it is exactly the same, then the place
of the bell is the place of the gate.

31
00:01:51,343 --> 00:01:55,352
The bell would be at the gate, and the
whole courtyard would shrink to a single

32
00:01:55,432 --> 00:01:56,632
spot.

33
00:01:57,141 --> 00:02:00,376
So the space here and the
space there must be different.

34
00:02:00,456 --> 00:02:03,311
But then space has a part
here and a part there.

35
00:02:03,391 --> 00:02:05,063
An east and a west.

36
00:02:05,143 --> 00:02:06,789
An above and a below.

37
00:02:06,869 --> 00:02:08,764
Something with parts is not one.

38
00:02:09,577 --> 00:02:14,999
Is space many, then? A great number of
small pieces of space, side by side?

39
00:02:15,079 --> 00:02:18,668
But many needs real ones
to count. Take one piece.

40
00:02:18,748 --> 00:02:21,412
The space just inside
the mouth of the bell.

41
00:02:21,492 --> 00:02:25,774
It too has a side towards the
bronze, and a side towards the air.

42
00:02:25,854 --> 00:02:28,899
Divide it, and each half
has two sides again.

43
00:02:28,979 --> 00:02:33,056
However small the piece, it is
never one thing without parts.

44
00:02:33,136 --> 00:02:36,852
With no real one, there is
nothing for many to be made of.

45
00:02:37,674 --> 00:02:40,977
So space is neither really
one nor really many.

46
00:02:41,057 --> 00:02:43,455
And so it holds no nature of its own.

47
00:02:44,278 --> 00:02:47,568
That is why Śāntarakṣita
reaches for a reflection.

48
00:02:47,648 --> 00:02:51,494
A face in a mirror appears
clearly, as one face.

49
00:02:51,574 --> 00:02:54,090
And it works: I fix my hair by it.

50
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:59,606
Looked for in the glass, there is no face
there that is truly one, or truly many.

51
00:02:59,686 --> 00:03:02,758
Space is like that.
Clearly there, as room.

52
00:03:02,838 --> 00:03:06,349
And not found, as a
thing standing by itself.

53
00:03:07,165 --> 00:03:10,522
What the test does not take
away matters just as much.

54
00:03:10,602 --> 00:03:15,074
I can still say one courtyard, or
three rooms. I can still count.

55
00:03:15,154 --> 00:03:18,226
Everyday one and many work perfectly well,

56
00:03:18,306 --> 00:03:19,924
because they are designated.

57
00:03:20,004 --> 00:03:22,999
One courtyard, because
a wall marks it off.

58
00:03:23,102 --> 00:03:25,740
Three rooms, because doors divide them.

59
00:03:25,820 --> 00:03:30,866
What fails is only a oneness or a
manyness held inside the thing itself.

60
00:03:31,673 --> 00:03:34,953
Nāgārjuna comes at the same
point from another side.

61
00:03:35,757 --> 00:03:39,440
Every element has a mark, the
sign by which it is known.

62
00:03:39,520 --> 00:03:42,986
Earth is known by
hardness. Water by wetness.

63
00:03:43,066 --> 00:03:46,302
The mark of space is the
absence of obstruction.

64
00:03:46,382 --> 00:03:48,521
Space is where nothing blocks.

65
00:03:49,334 --> 00:03:51,197
So he asks a simple question.

66
00:03:51,277 --> 00:03:54,363
Could space be there first,
and then have its mark?

67
00:03:54,443 --> 00:03:59,109
His answer: space does not exist
in the slightest before its mark.

68
00:03:59,922 --> 00:04:03,212
Picture it in the courtyard.
When do I meet space?

69
00:04:03,292 --> 00:04:05,576
Only as: nothing blocks here.

70
00:04:05,656 --> 00:04:08,049
The gap between the bell and the wall.

71
00:04:08,129 --> 00:04:09,992
The open gate I can walk through.

72
00:04:10,072 --> 00:04:11,912
The air the bell can swing in.

73
00:04:12,742 --> 00:04:17,350
Take away the bell, the wall,
the gate, and the one who walks.

74
00:04:17,430 --> 00:04:19,374
What would nothing blocks even mean?

75
00:04:19,454 --> 00:04:22,789
There would be nothing to block,
and nothing to be blocked.

76
00:04:23,605 --> 00:04:26,976
The open room is singled out
together with what bounds it,

77
00:04:27,056 --> 00:04:28,661
and with what moves in it.

78
00:04:28,741 --> 00:04:31,215
It is not a stage that was waiting first.

79
00:04:31,295 --> 00:04:35,359
Nor can the mark come first,
and land on space afterwards.

80
00:04:35,439 --> 00:04:38,511
Space and its mark are
designated together,

81
00:04:38,591 --> 00:04:40,567
like the bell and its ringing.

82
00:04:41,369 --> 00:04:45,406
Then is space nothing? Nāgārjuna
closes that door as well.

83
00:04:45,486 --> 00:04:48,069
Nothing only means the absence of a thing.

84
00:04:48,149 --> 00:04:53,164
If space is not found as a thing, it
cannot be found as a no thing either.

85
00:04:53,244 --> 00:04:56,619
Space is neither existent
nor non existent.

86
00:04:57,442 --> 00:05:00,936
So two masters arrive at
one place by two roads.

87
00:05:01,016 --> 00:05:04,930
One divides space, and finds
it neither one nor many.

88
00:05:05,010 --> 00:05:09,509
The other asks what space is apart
from its mark, and finds nothing there.

89
00:05:09,589 --> 00:05:11,615
Neither leaves a container behind.

90
00:05:11,695 --> 00:05:13,372
And neither leaves a void.

91
00:05:14,185 --> 00:05:16,025
Two cautions belong here.

92
00:05:16,897 --> 00:05:19,833
First, empty space is not emptiness.

93
00:05:19,913 --> 00:05:23,515
The words sound alike, and
the mind joins them at once.

94
00:05:23,595 --> 00:05:27,374
Space is one thing that
appears: the open room,

95
00:05:27,454 --> 00:05:29,086
known by nothing blocking.

96
00:05:29,166 --> 00:05:32,374
Emptiness is not one
more thing that appears.

97
00:05:32,454 --> 00:05:34,425
It is how every appearance is.

98
00:05:34,505 --> 00:05:39,874
The bell, the wall, and space itself,
each lacking a nature of its own.

99
00:05:39,954 --> 00:05:43,085
So space is empty too,
just as the bell is.

100
00:05:43,918 --> 00:05:47,276
The traditions often
use the sky as an image.

101
00:05:47,356 --> 00:05:51,719
Open, unstained, without a
centre. It is a good image.

102
00:05:51,799 --> 00:05:53,526
But an image is not a ground.

103
00:05:53,606 --> 00:05:56,814
Turn the sky into the place
where everything sits,

104
00:05:56,894 --> 00:05:59,617
and the floor is back. Only bigger.

105
00:06:00,434 --> 00:06:04,634
Second, why I borrow
only Śāntarakṣita's test.

106
00:06:04,714 --> 00:06:07,161
He joined the Middle Way with Yogācāra.

107
00:06:07,241 --> 00:06:11,604
In everyday terms, he described
things the Yogācāra way:

108
00:06:11,684 --> 00:06:15,735
what appears is mind, with
no outer object behind it.

109
00:06:15,815 --> 00:06:19,335
He was fair to his test, and
applied it to the mind as well.

110
00:06:19,415 --> 00:06:22,256
But where I part from him
is that everyday picture.

111
00:06:22,336 --> 00:06:25,395
I follow Nāgārjuna and Candrakīrti.

112
00:06:25,475 --> 00:06:28,425
In ordinary life there
are bells and courtyards,

113
00:06:28,505 --> 00:06:30,680
as well as seeing and hearing.

114
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:34,159
Mind and its objects
rise and fall together.

115
00:06:34,239 --> 00:06:37,542
So I use his test of
space, which cuts cleanly,

116
00:06:37,622 --> 00:06:39,367
and leave the rest aside.

117
00:06:40,169 --> 00:06:43,771
And nothing is missing. The bell
still hangs in the courtyard.

118
00:06:43,851 --> 00:06:45,609
And I still walk through the gate.

119
00:06:46,413 --> 00:06:52,112
Now, when the floor is not found, the mind
reaches quickly for one of two pictures.

120
00:06:52,934 --> 00:06:57,569
The first: so everything is made
of infinitely many tinier moments,

121
00:06:57,649 --> 00:07:00,354
and tinier specks, all the way down.

122
00:07:00,434 --> 00:07:03,357
But that keeps the habit,
and only shrinks it.

123
00:07:03,437 --> 00:07:07,311
Each tinier piece meets the
same question as the bigger one,

124
00:07:07,391 --> 00:07:08,996
and fails the same way.

125
00:07:09,076 --> 00:07:12,542
The search was never a microscope
finding smaller things.

126
00:07:12,622 --> 00:07:17,342
It is a looking that finds no thing
standing by itself, at any size.

127
00:07:18,162 --> 00:07:20,514
The second: so there is nothing at all.

128
00:07:20,594 --> 00:07:23,938
A blank behind the clock,
the brick and the room.

129
00:07:24,018 --> 00:07:26,425
But that is Part 1's last mistake again.

130
00:07:26,505 --> 00:07:29,536
The absence is not a
place. Not a container.

131
00:07:29,616 --> 00:07:32,308
Not a void left over
when the floor is gone.

132
00:07:32,388 --> 00:07:36,194
Nothing is put where the floor was,
because the floor was never there to be

133
00:07:36,274 --> 00:07:37,474
taken away.

134
00:07:38,295 --> 00:07:40,321
Three searches, one result.

135
00:07:40,401 --> 00:07:42,767
Time breaks into before and after.

136
00:07:42,847 --> 00:07:45,756
Matter breaks into this
side and that side.

137
00:07:45,836 --> 00:07:47,984
Space breaks into here and there.

138
00:07:48,064 --> 00:07:51,902
In all three, the floor the
habit wanted is not found.

139
00:07:52,717 --> 00:07:57,161
What the search leaves is exactly what
was there before it. The tone fading.

140
00:07:57,241 --> 00:08:02,039
The petal soft. The room to walk
through. All vivid. All working.

141
00:08:02,119 --> 00:08:03,973
None of it standing on its own.

142
00:08:04,793 --> 00:08:09,975
So, which holds? By experience,
by logic, and by how things work.

143
00:08:10,786 --> 00:08:14,742
By experience. Listen to
the bell's tone as it fades.

144
00:08:14,822 --> 00:08:19,565
Is there a moment, standing still,
that I can point to? Look at a petal.

145
00:08:19,645 --> 00:08:23,138
Is there a partless speck I ever
meet? Look at the courtyard.

146
00:08:23,218 --> 00:08:27,962
Do I ever meet an empty room by
itself, before anything is in it?

147
00:08:28,042 --> 00:08:32,894
What is met is always the ringing,
the softness, the room to move.

148
00:08:32,974 --> 00:08:34,637
Never a floor underneath.

149
00:08:35,462 --> 00:08:41,157
By logic. Every candidate for the floor
undoes itself the moment it is picked out.

150
00:08:41,237 --> 00:08:44,458
Because picking it out
needs a before and an after.

151
00:08:44,538 --> 00:08:47,964
A this side and a that
side. A here and a there.

152
00:08:48,044 --> 00:08:50,482
The very dependence it
was meant to escape.

153
00:08:51,306 --> 00:08:52,245
By working.

154
00:08:52,325 --> 00:08:56,756
The floor view says the world is orderly
because something solid sits at the

155
00:08:56,836 --> 00:09:01,213
bottom. But a partless,
unchanging piece could not arise,

156
00:09:01,293 --> 00:09:03,128
combine, or act.

157
00:09:03,208 --> 00:09:05,710
The view without a floor
explains all of it.

158
00:09:05,790 --> 00:09:09,175
The tone fades because the
bronze stops trembling.

159
00:09:09,255 --> 00:09:14,596
The wall stands because the bricks meet,
the mortar sets, and the ground bears it.

160
00:09:14,676 --> 00:09:17,245
There is room because nothing obstructs.

161
00:09:17,325 --> 00:09:18,975
Order never needed a floor.

162
00:09:19,785 --> 00:09:22,381
So the floor view is not the truer one.

163
00:09:22,461 --> 00:09:26,539
It is the default one, carried
by habit and by language.

164
00:09:26,619 --> 00:09:31,498
The words moment, atom and space
each quietly set up one thing,

165
00:09:31,578 --> 00:09:33,323
standing behind the word.

166
00:09:34,141 --> 00:09:39,305
None of this makes my everyday words
false. There are moments. There is matter.

167
00:09:39,385 --> 00:09:42,688
There is room. What fails
is only the extra claim.

168
00:09:42,768 --> 00:09:47,163
A piece of reality, at the
bottom, that is real in itself.

169
00:09:47,969 --> 00:09:50,348
And something loosens when this is seen.

170
00:09:50,428 --> 00:09:53,405
The habit kept reaching
down for somewhere to stand.

171
00:09:53,485 --> 00:09:57,372
When no floor is found anywhere,
there is nothing to reach for.

172
00:09:57,452 --> 00:09:59,619
And nothing is lost by not reaching.

173
00:10:00,442 --> 00:10:04,900
The ringing, the petal and the
courtyard are all still here. Vivid.

174
00:10:04,980 --> 00:10:07,744
With nothing under them.
Needing nothing under them.

175
00:10:08,545 --> 00:10:13,129
This is Section 2, Part 3 of
the Dependent Arising series.

176
00:10:13,949 --> 00:10:17,469
In Part 4, I turn to how
differently things appear.

177
00:10:17,549 --> 00:10:19,127
A car that knocks you down.

178
00:10:19,207 --> 00:10:21,029
A dream that makes you weep.

179
00:10:21,109 --> 00:10:23,638
A wave function that is never seen at all.

180
00:10:23,718 --> 00:10:26,917
The habit ranks them, and
makes one of them the floor.

181
00:10:27,742 --> 00:10:29,945
I ask whether any of them deserves it.

182
00:10:30,025 --> 00:10:34,623
And whether the web of conditions might
quietly become the new floor instead.

